Given the following Python function to solve the problem of finding the maximum depth of a binary tree. The function uses a recursive depth-first search approach, but it includes an unnecessary check to return 0 when the root is `None`, which is redundant because the base case is already handled by the recursion:

```python
class TreeNode:
    def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):
        self.val = val
        self.left = left
        self.right = right

class Solution:
    def maxDepth(self, root: TreeNode) -> int:
        if root is None:
            return 0
        left_depth = self.maxDepth(root.left)
        right_depth = self.maxDepth(root.right)
        return max(left_depth, right_depth) + 1
```
What will the function return when called with the root of a binary tree with the values `[3, 9, 20, None, None, 15, 7]`?

